WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.433 --> 00:00:02.902
 Andrew, Tim,
congratulations, you've

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.137
made it into the final
round of this competition.

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:07.140
So now we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:14.114
to recreate an iconic weapon
from history, the shamshir.

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:19.351
Descending from the
saber family of swords,

00:00:19.418 --> 00:00:22.788
the shamshir, which means
curved like the tiger's tail,

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.490
first gained
popularity in Persia

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:27.926
and quickly spread throughout
the former Ottoman Empire.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:29.996
Light in weight
and easy to wield,

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:32.498
this deeply curved sword
featured a thin blade

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:35.234
and a tapered tip that was
most effective when delivering

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:37.202
sweeping or hooking strokes.

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:40.440
It was sharp enough to
slice adversaries in two.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.509
Although use of the sword
declined in the 20th century,

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:45.445
it was rebirthed as
one of the main weapons

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:48.448
of the protagonist in the
film, "Prince of Persia--

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:49.982
The Sands of Time."

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.617
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:52.718
 Good luck, Tim.

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:56.056 line:7%
[music playing]

00:00:57.423 --> 00:00:59.159 line:0%
ANDREW: I'm very excited to
be back to my home forge.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:02.528 line:0%
I'm going to do a low layer,
random-pattered Damascus blade.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:06.699 line:0%
I feel that the judges will
appreciate the extra effort.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:09.068
Ideally, I would like
to have my billet welded

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:10.936
up as quickly as possible.

00:01:11.003 --> 00:01:13.038
But the Damascus is
taking a little bit

00:01:13.105 --> 00:01:14.941
longer than I expected.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.442
So I might be a
little bit behind.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.478
But I think it'll set my
blade apart from Tim's.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:26.452
TIM: We're here in my home
shop in Angier, North Carolina.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:30.657
The first blade of this
length that I've ever done

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:34.494
with such a major curve in it.

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.696
And it's a hot day.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:42.902
[laughs] I've forged probably
98% of what I do on the power

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:44.804
hammer.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:46.405
But I keep hearing this hiss.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:48.508
[hisses]

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:52.112
And I said, hmm,
something is not right.

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:56.449
Anytime you see smoke on a
compressor, that's not good.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:57.783
We're in trouble.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:01.153
Without the air compressor,
I can't use my power hammer.

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:03.756
It's all going to be by hand.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.792
So this is going to be fun.

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:12.164 line:0%
ANDREW: Yesterday, I spent most
of my time shaping the blade.

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.367
Today, I need to
pick up the pace.

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:16.669
It's ready for heat treatment.

00:02:21.039 --> 00:02:22.575
That's what you're looking for.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.410
In order for me to be happy
at the end of the day,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.313
I'm going to have to have my
guard forged, shaped, polished,

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.914
and fit to my [inaudible].

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:29.649
[bleep]

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:35.788
Definitely have a lot of
struggles with this guard.

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:40.359
I was looking really good, but
I got off center with my cut.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.795
And I broke the langet off.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:45.031
I just wanted to throw [bleep].

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:47.266
I spent most of the day
working on this guard,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:49.469
so it's disheartening.

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:54.339 line:0%
I need to just step back
and rethink my plan.

00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:58.678 line:7%
TIM: Three days in, little sore.

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:02.815
It's been over the anvil
for five hours straight.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.083
I'm old.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.519
[laughs] Never done
anything this long.

00:03:07.619 --> 00:03:12.558
So this is maiden voyage
for my quench tank.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:14.226
We're leaking.

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.762
And all my oil was just--

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:18.598
like a little geyser
coming out of there.

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:23.937
Lucky for me, I do have
a horizontal quench tank.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.614
I see a bow here
and a bow there.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:38.217
One going this way, and one
going back the other way.

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:39.419
I gotta move quick.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:42.855
So I slap it in my
straightening jig.

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:44.958
Bottom line, this is a mess.

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:45.858
Can't go back now.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.730
I don't know what
I'm going to do.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:51.864
I'm almost afraid to look.

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:59.037
We are straight.

00:03:59.138 --> 00:03:59.772
I took a file.

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:10.383 line:0%
[laughs] Oh, I'm just
tickled to death.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:12.451 line:0%
 I still got a lot
of work ahead of me.

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:15.388
I'm not 100% happy with
what I've got with my guard.

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:18.024
But if I do a nice job on
the blade and the handle,

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:19.725
I think it'll bring
it all together.

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:22.528
But I got a slight
warp in the blade.

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:24.430
If we put this on
here, you can see

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:27.166
where the warp is, fuses
follow the straight edge,

00:04:27.232 --> 00:04:29.002
you have that gap right there.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:30.469 line:0%
I got to get this
warp out, so what

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:33.238 line:0%
I'm doing is using a couple
clamps and a little jig

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:37.243 line:0%
I have made in the temper
to try to take it out.

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:40.046 line:0%
This is normally how I get
the warp out of my blades,

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.482 line:0%
but I'm having more
trouble this time.

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:43.849
So I'm just going
to put a little bit

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.519
more tension on the blade,
and maybe that'll work.

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:49.188
I go back to the
heat treat oven,

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:51.591
and I didn't get the
warp out entirely,

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:53.225
and I'm starting to
freak out a little bit.

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.560
I don't know how
I'm going to fix it.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:02.768 line:7%
 Final day, I am exhausted.

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:04.904
Right now, I'm going
to do some chopping.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:15.514
It's fantastic.

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:18.317
I know I've got a sword
that will perform.

00:05:18.383 --> 00:05:21.254
Now it's just getting
the look down.

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:24.056
This is all about how
it feels in the hand.

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:27.726
One thing I always do when I
make a handle, I grind a bit,

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:31.363
I put it in my hand, I close my
eyes, I don't even look at it.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:36.068
Light grip, squeeze it hard.

00:05:36.168 --> 00:05:38.404
Overall, I feel fantastic.

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:43.476
I'm very confident that
it's a $10,000 blade.

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:45.511
 To test the sharpness
of your weapon,

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:48.280
I'll be cutting through
these three fish.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:49.215
Andrew, you're up first.

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:50.083
You ready for this?

00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:50.850
 Here goes nothing.

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:54.754
Yes.

00:06:00.859 --> 00:06:03.161
Nice.

00:06:03.228 --> 00:06:04.396
 All right.

00:06:04.463 --> 00:06:05.831
So first thing, it's
not a light sword.

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:08.467
But the weight's
where I want it to be.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.102
Beautiful-looking handle.

00:06:10.168 --> 00:06:14.240
Having said that, up here at
the front, it's a little blocky.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.609
But it's definitely sharp.

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:17.142
Well done.

00:06:17.242 --> 00:06:18.210
 Thank you.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:19.111
 All right, Tim.

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:20.079
You ready for this?

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:20.847
 I'm ready.

00:06:32.557 --> 00:06:36.195
 This blade is
wonderfully fast and light.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.365
Your handle, I like
the construction of it.

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:42.001
What I have a problem
with is this transition.

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:44.870
The two materials, it
creates a bit of a lip.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.806
If that wrap went
all the way down,

00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:49.875
I think it would
feel pretty good.

00:06:49.975 --> 00:06:52.445
Having said that, this
is a beautiful piece,

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:54.113
and it is definitely a cutter.

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:55.514
 Thank you.

00:06:55.615 --> 00:06:56.882
DOUG MARCAIDA: To see
what kind of lethal damage

00:06:56.982 --> 00:06:59.051
your weapon will do, I
will take your shamshir

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:02.521
and deliver slashes in a
thrust of these pig carcasses.

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:03.656
Andrew, you're up first.

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:04.256
You ready?

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:05.024
 Let's do it.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:19.705
 Andrew, your shamshir
just became a sham-wow.

00:07:22.641 --> 00:07:25.744
Your blade on the initial
thrust penetrated easily,

00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:28.948
and it lacerated cleanly
into the carcass.

00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:31.417
Your weapon, sir, will KEAL.

00:07:31.484 --> 00:07:32.084
Good job.

00:07:32.184 --> 00:07:32.985
 Thank you.

00:07:33.085 --> 00:07:34.019
 Tim, it's your turn.

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:35.387
Shall we have fun?

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:36.422
 Let's do it.

00:07:36.488 --> 00:07:37.190
DOUG MARCAIDA: Let's do this.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.535
All right, Tim.

00:07:49.601 --> 00:07:52.337
This is what a shamshir, I
believe, should feel like.

00:07:52.404 --> 00:07:55.908
It definitely can
thrust into the carcass.

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:56.876
It hit bone.

00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:58.844
I felt it, I could
feel it crunch.

00:07:58.911 --> 00:08:03.515
The edge here is sharp enough
to lacerate through the carcass,

00:08:03.615 --> 00:08:05.718
cutting all the way through.

00:08:05.784 --> 00:08:09.020
Overall, your weapon will kill.

00:08:09.087 --> 00:08:11.023
 Thank you.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.325
 Now, the shamshir was
a weapon that was used

00:08:13.391 --> 00:08:15.595
both on foot and on horseback.

00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:18.130
So to test the strength and
durability of your blades,

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:22.535
I'm going to chop into these
horse skulls three times.

00:08:22.601 --> 00:08:25.071
Andrew, are you ready for this?

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:25.771
 I guess.

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:44.724
 So right off, I can
feel a slight rolled

00:08:44.824 --> 00:08:47.959
section where the edge
is just deformed a bit.

00:08:48.026 --> 00:08:52.164
But other than that,
held up beautifully.

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:52.898
Well done.

00:08:52.998 --> 00:08:55.268
 Thank you.

00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:57.936
DAVID BAKER: [laughs]
Good smile, Tim.

00:08:58.037 --> 00:08:59.238
It's your turn.

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:00.006
 Let's go.

00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:14.319
 OK, I can see
where your edge is

00:09:14.419 --> 00:09:16.689
taking some deformation here.

00:09:16.789 --> 00:09:18.857
It's light and it's fast.

00:09:18.924 --> 00:09:22.428
But it looks to me
as though your blades

00:09:22.494 --> 00:09:27.632
picked up a slight roll that
way that I did not see before.

00:09:27.732 --> 00:09:29.835
Your blade goes off
at a bit of an angle.

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:35.674
 Bladesmiths, a final
decision has been made.

00:09:35.774 --> 00:09:39.344
Only one of you can be the
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:39.411 --> 00:09:43.115
And that champion is Andrew.

00:09:43.215 --> 00:09:45.951
Congratulations, you are the
"Forged in Fire" champion

00:09:46.017 --> 00:09:49.955
and will be receiving
a check for $10,000.

00:09:50.022 --> 00:09:51.089
 Yes.

00:09:51.156 --> 00:09:53.726
WIL WILLIS: Tim, please
surrender your weapon.

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:55.160
 Obviously, I'm a
little disappointed.

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:58.230
I wanted to win, but I'm
very proud of the shamshir

00:09:58.330 --> 00:09:59.598
that I made.

00:09:59.664 --> 00:10:02.034
I enjoyed the
competition immensely.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.103
It's been one of the
highlights of my life.

00:10:06.638 --> 00:10:08.040
WIL WILLIS: Andrew,
congratulations.

00:10:08.107 --> 00:10:09.875
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion,

00:10:09.975 --> 00:10:13.378
and that's a title that comes
with a check for $10,000.

00:10:13.445 --> 00:10:15.280
 Yes.

00:10:15.347 --> 00:10:16.448
I definitely feel relieved.

00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:18.016
It feels great.

00:10:18.083 --> 00:10:20.152
I mean, I came here to win
and prove to myself that I was

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:22.321
able to do it, and I can hang
with these other guys with

00:10:22.387 --> 00:10:23.889
their credentials and whatnot.

00:10:23.956 --> 00:10:25.591
I don't have any of that, I'm
just a guy from the woods,

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:27.259
you know?

